<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wujing Zongyao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1044">1044</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Complete Essentials for the Military Classics</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wujing Zongyao (Complete Essentials for the Military Classics) is a Chinese military compendium compiled between 1040 and 1044 by Zeng Gongliang, Ding Du and Yang Weide on the order of Emperor Renzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. Covering strategy, fortification, weapons and naval warfare, it contains the earliest known written formulas for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, given for incendiary and smoke bombs launched by catapult, and describes a fish-shaped magnetised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wujing-zongyao.jpg" alt="Wujing Zongyao" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PericlesofAthens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_Gunpowder_Formula.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Emperor Renzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> sponsored the compendium because he was concerned that many officials were unfamiliar with the military classics, and partly in response to the Song dynasty's war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> of Western Xia. He also wrote its preface. The chief editor, Zeng Gongliang, was a government official rather than a general, and the historian Lorge takes this to mean the book was likely written for other officials. Much was copied from older works whose authors go unnamed; Ralph D. Sawyer calls it &quot;essentially a cut-and-paste job&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book divides Chinese warships into six categories, among them tower ships, covered swoopers and sea hawk ships, a typology that reappeared in naval texts for centuries. Its illustrations of ships were copied into the Wubei Zhi of about 1628 and into Japanese works as late as Kanazawa Kanemitsu's Wakan Senyōshū of 1766.</p><p>Its three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> formulas are for a bomb launched from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchet</a>, a similar bomb with hooks to catch on wooden structures, and a poison-smoke bomb. The low-nitrate powder of the time burned rather than exploded, and the book also describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-arrow">fire arrows</a> shot from bows or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and the swallow-tail incendiary lowered onto besiegers from city walls. It gives the oldest illustration of a Chinese flamethrower, a brass tank with a double-acting piston pump that shot a continuous blast of flame, with instructions for its use and repair. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>, a thin leaf of iron cut like a fish was heated red-hot, quenched with its tail pointing north and floated on water, so that its head pointed south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>It is one of 347 military treatises the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> lists for the Song period, and one of very few to survive. The original copy in the Imperial Library was lost when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1126, and only a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> remained, since the government allowed few people to read it. A new edition was made in 1231, and the whole work was reprinted in 1510, the oldest copy extant, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> judged the most faithful to the original. Late in the Ming dynasty Fan Jingwen wrote a continuation, the Xu Wujing Zongyao, because he felt the reprints in circulation were out of date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wujing Zongyao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New weapons partly made up for this: Song armies developed and used gunpowder weapons, including fire arrows, bombs and early fire lances, well before they appeared elsewhere, and the formula for gunpowder was first recorded in the military compendium <b>Wujing Zongyao</b> of 1044.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest surviving formulas for it appear in the <b>Wujing Zongyao</b>, a military compendium completed for the Song court in 1044.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
